On 10/07/2014 05:41 PM, Ed Merkle wrote:
> Thanks, I incorrectly assumed that fixed.x would exclude sch from the
> observed variables.

I can understand the confusion. The 'fixed.x' flag only determines if we
will estimate (as free parameters) the intercepts, variances and
covariances of the exogenous covariates. It does not imply that we
'condition' on x.

As it happens, I am thinking of adding a 'conditional.x' flag to lavaan,
so that we can 'condition on x' if we want to, even in the continuous
case. This would imply that we first regress x out, and then proceed
with the (residualized) y only. This is already the case when some y are
categorical, but not if all y are continuous.

> But, in my case, it is probably more intuitive to treat sch as a
> grouping variable, with some parameters constrained to be equal across
> the two groups.  While the above models and data wouldn't indicate it,
> these issues are relevant to framing a repeated measures ANOVA as an SEM.

Sure. The multigroup approach would avoid the homogeneity assumption of
ANOVA and friends.

Yves.

