     I was demonstrating missing data procedures for my class this week.  Of course, I demo’ed the FIML option in lavaan, but I also wanted to demo multiple imputation via runMI.  The documentation, as I understand it, says that the chi=”all” option will produce multiple chi-squares, based on different approaches to combining across imputations.  But when I run it (see attached transcript and attached synthetic dataset), I only get one chi-square.  Am I misunderstanding the documentation, or am I doing something wrong?

     Sadly, the very notion of missing data procedures beyond pairwise deletion or mean-plugging (ick!) was a revelation to this class of business Ph.D. students, who have been through several quant methods courses before they get to mine.  Somewhere, the word is not getting out that there are better solutions.

     Thanks.

--Ed Rigdon

From udd77bbde7a69fe72 at groups.google.com  Fri Oct 10 14:27:08 2014
From: udd77bbde7a69fe72 at groups.google.com (kretzsc...@gmail.com)
Date: Fri, 10 Oct 2014 14:27:08 +0000
Subject: Residuals of a latent regression to predict further variables / higher order factor identification
Message-ID: <ezx9ISQRBpA.Xmp2S_bH68kJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/ezx9ISQRBpA/m/Xmp2S_bH68kJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi lavaan fellows,

I want to use the residuals of a latent regression to predict a third variable, that is:

- Variable 1 predicts Variable 2
- Residuals of Variable 2 (i.e., withouth the influence of Variable 1) and Variable 1 predict Variable 3

It is sometimes used in the literature, for example in Wüstenberg et al. (2012), page 9-10, Figure 5 (see attachment). They used a MPlus syntax like this one:

F1 by ind11 ind12 ind13;
F2 by ind21 ind22 ind23;
F3 by ind31 ind32 ind33;

F2 on F1;
F2_residual by F2;
F2@0;
F2_residual with F1@0;

F3 on F2_residual F1;

I would adapt it to lavaan in this way:

F1 =~ ind11 ind12 ind13
F2 =~ ind21 ind22 ind23
F3 =~ ind31 ind32 ind33

F2 ~ F1
F2_residual =~ F2
F2 ~~ 0*F2
F2_residual ~~ 0*F1

F3 ~ F2_residual F1

Would you agree?

Well, actually my Factor 2 is a higher order factor of two latent factors. According to Brown (2006), page 205, Figure 5.3, and some thoughts about identification I would model Factor 2 in this way:

F2.1 =~ NA*ind21 ind22 ind23
F2.2 =~ NA*ind24 ind25 ind26
F2.1 ~~ 1*F2.1
F2.2 ~~ 1*F2.2

F2 =~ NA*F2.1 + a*F2.1 + a*F2.2
F2 ~~ 1*F2

That is, the factor loadings of the latent factors to the higher order factor are constrained to equality for identification issues. Still okay?

Okay, now there is a problem with the combined syntax:

# measurement models
F2.1 =~ NA*ind21 ind22 ind23
F2.2 =~ NA*ind24 ind25 ind26
F2.1 ~~ 1*F2.1
F2.2 ~~ 1*F2.2
F2 =~ NA*F2.1 + a*F2.1 + a*F2.2
F2 ~~ 1*F2

F1 =~ ind11 ind12 ind13
F3 =~ ind31 ind32 ind33

# structural models
F2 ~ F1
F2_residual =~ F2
F2 ~~ 0*F2
F2_residual ~~ 0*F1

F3 ~ F2_residual F1

It produces this error message:

> Error in lavParseModelString(model) :
>   lavaan ERROR: duplicate model element in: F2~~0*F2

I understand the problem (F2 ~~1*F2 vs F2~~0*F2) somehow, but I do not know a solution. Is there another possibilty to model Factor 2 in a way that I can skip F2 ~~1*F2? Or is there another way to use the residuals of Factor 2?

Best,
André

References:
Brown, T. A. (2006). Confirmatory factor analysis for applied research. New York: Guilford Press.
Wüstenberg, S., Greiff, S., & Funke, J. (2012). Complex problem solving — More than reasoning? Intelligence, 40, 1–14. doi:10.1016/j.intell.2011.11.003

